
### `LICENSE`

Se vuoi mantenere il progetto **privato**, non è necessario inserire una licenza open source.

Se invece il repository sarà pubblico ma vuoi **impedire che altri possano riutilizzare liberamente articoli, database, template e contenuti**, non userei una classica MIT License.

Per questo progetto sceglierei inizialmente una licenza proprietaria molto semplice:

```text
Copyright (c) 2026 Your Daily English Lesson

All rights reserved.

This repository and its contents, including but not limited to
articles, editorial databases, YAML files, templates, prompts,
images, website content, and other original materials, are
protected by applicable copyright laws.

No part of this repository may be copied, reproduced, modified,
redistributed, republished, or used for commercial purposes
without prior written permission from the copyright holder.

The source code may be made available for viewing or development
purposes, but no rights are granted to reproduce or redistribute
the original content.

For permission requests, please contact the repository owner.
